  "metadata": {
    "title": "SpaceX's trading debut puts the market on notice",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Yahoo Finance Senior Reporter Brooke DiPalma discusses Opening Bid's Stock of the Day,\" SpaceX (SPCX) as the company begins to reshape the space industry. Joined by B. Riley Wealth Chief Market Strategist Art Hogan, Yahoo Finance Senior Business Reporter Ines Ferré, and Markets & Data Editor Jared Blikre.\nThe fact that they were able to get this out and and uh with very few bumps in the road. You didn't hear about any disasters, any halting of trading, all the things that could go along with the massive amount of volume that was introduced to the market says that the plumbing is working well. The fact that there was an audience for this and and demand was, you know, depending on who you believe, two to four times oversubscribed is is important as well because there's a lot of large cap names that are in waiting in the wings to IPO themselves. I think the fact that this went 20% retail is very telling of the fan base or the the disciples of Elon Musk that are willing to believe that in 2030, the revenue picture will look much different than it does today. As a matter of fact, he's projecting, Elon Musk is that he's going to double revenues every year for the next five years. So, it's a belief in what Elon Musk can do and that's why there's such a large cohort of retail investors that are also Tesla investors.\nYeah, look, I mean investors are used to Elon Musk um putting projections out there and lofty projections. I mean, keep in mind that more than a year ago, Elon Musk had talked about Tesla reaching a valuation of somewhere around 25 trillion. So keep that in mind that these projections are what Elon Musk puts out there as far as where the company is going and that's part of the vision. That's what these buyer these there the retail investors and the rest of investors that are in this stock are really buying. But if you take a look at what the street is expecting. I mean, you got Goldman Sachs that's saying that revenue by 2030, uh coming in at 470 billion. Um that's because of AI infrastructure and data delivery. You've got Morgan Stanley uh that is projecting somewhere around 330 billion and New Street 195 billion. So really different sort of projections when it comes to that 2030 timeline.\nFirst of all, I I agree with Art 100%. There were so many things that could have gone gone wrong with the supersized IPO. It came off very, very smoothly. So that's a win for the Nasdaq. And just, you know, getting that out of the way, Vanda Track, they track retail investors. They said that SpaceX is trading like a retail Megacap on day one. So that was Friday. They brought in $117 million. There you go. You can see on your screen how this compares to Coinbase back in 2021, Uber back in 20 uh 2019. Uh if you take a look at the dates down there, all these IPOs that did not attract nearly as much volume, we haven't had that many recently. Uh in fact, in this latest bull market that began in late 2022, I'm only seeing Arm down there in the bottom row. So there's a huge response here and this was very uncharacteristic the way the retail trade has leaned over the last few years.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Yahoo Finance Senior Reporter Brooke DiPalma discusses Opening Bid's Stock of the Day,\" SpaceX (SPCX) as the company begins to reshape the space industry. Joined by B. Riley Wealth Chief Market Strategist Art Hogan, Yahoo Finance Senior Business Reporter Ines Ferré, and Markets & Data Editor Jared Blikre.",
      "The fact that they were able to get this out and and uh with very few bumps in the road. You didn't hear about any disasters, any halting of trading, all the things that could go along with the massive amount of volume that was introduced to the market says that the plumbing is working well. The fact that there was an audience for this and and demand was, you know, depending on who you believe, two to four times oversubscribed is is important as well because there's a lot of large cap names that are in waiting in the wings to IPO themselves. I think the fact that this went 20% retail is very telling of the fan base or the the disciples of Elon Musk that are willing to believe that in 2030, the revenue picture will look much different than it does today. As a matter of fact, he's projecting, Elon Musk is that he's going to double revenues every year for the next five years. So, it's a belief in what Elon Musk can do and that's why there's such a large cohort of retail investors that are also Tesla investors.",
      "Yeah, look, I mean investors are used to Elon Musk um putting projections out there and lofty projections. I mean, keep in mind that more than a year ago, Elon Musk had talked about Tesla reaching a valuation of somewhere around 25 trillion. So keep that in mind that these projections are what Elon Musk puts out there as far as where the company is going and that's part of the vision. That's what these buyer these there the retail investors and the rest of investors that are in this stock are really buying. But if you take a look at what the street is expecting. I mean, you got Goldman Sachs that's saying that revenue by 2030, uh coming in at 470 billion. Um that's because of AI infrastructure and data delivery. You've got Morgan Stanley uh that is projecting somewhere around 330 billion and New Street 195 billion. So really different sort of projections when it comes to that 2030 timeline.",
      "First of all, I I agree with Art 100%. There were so many things that could have gone gone wrong with the supersized IPO. It came off very, very smoothly. So that's a win for the Nasdaq. And just, you know, getting that out of the way, Vanda Track, they track retail investors. They said that SpaceX is trading like a retail Megacap on day one. So that was Friday. They brought in $117 million. There you go. You can see on your screen how this compares to Coinbase back in 2021, Uber back in 20 uh 2019. Uh if you take a look at the dates down there, all these IPOs that did not attract nearly as much volume, we haven't had that many recently. Uh in fact, in this latest bull market that began in late 2022, I'm only seeing Arm down there in the bottom row. So there's a huge response here and this was very uncharacteristic the way the retail trade has leaned over the last few years."
